ormat explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nQ: 2, 10: 1, 6: 1, 4: 3, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\npair: ♠5 ♦5\npair: ♥8 ♦8\npair: ♣Q ♦Q\nsingle: ♠3\nsingle: ♥4\nsingle: ♦5\nsingle: ♥7\nsingle: ♥8\nsingle: ♠9\nsingle: ♥10\nsingle: ♣Q\nsingle: ♦K\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nSince this is a Free Play (no last play to beat), I must play something. Let me analyze the situation:\n\n**Current Position:**\n- I have 12 cards\n- Landlord (next player/enemy) has 4 cards - VERY CLOSE TO WINNING\n- Teammate (previous player) has 5 cards\n\n**Key Observations:**\n1. The landlord only has 4 cards left - this is critical! I need to prevent them from winning.\n2. My teammate has 5 cards and has been playing actively (just played singles: ♦A, ♣K, ♠10)\n3. I need to either gain control or help my teammate gain control\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n1. **Play ♦K (single)** - This is my highest single card. Playing high cards now could help maintain control and prevent the landlord from easily taking the lead.\n\n2. **Play a pair (♣Q ♦Q, ♥8 ♦8, or ♠5 ♦5)** - Pairs are harder to beat and could force the landlord to use valuable cards.\n\n3. **Play a low single (♠3, ♥4)** - Conserve high cards, but risks giving landlord easy control.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- With the landlord at 4 cards, I cannot afford to give them easy control with a weak play\n- Playing ♦K is strong but uses my highest single\n- Playing a pair (especially ♣Q ♦Q) is strategic because:\n  * Only 2 Queens remain unknown (likely the landlord has them or none)\n  * Pairs are harder to beat than singles\n  * Forces the landlord to respond with a pair or pass\n  * Preserves my King for later\n\nReason:\nI choose to play the pair ♣Q ♦Q because with the landlord holding only 4 cards, I need to apply maximum pressure. A pair is more restrictive than a single card and forces the landlord to either use a higher pair (limiting their options) or pass control back to my teammate who has been playing well. This protects against the landlord easily winning while keeping my King (highest single) in reserve.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣Q", "♦Q"]}'}
